## Motivation

The paper addresses the challenge of asking clarification questions in conversational agent design. While large language models (LLMs) have shown remarkable capabilities in two-need conversations involving task-oriented clarification or retrieval-related clarification, the effectiveness of these agents in the broader range of human-computer interactions (HCI) is underexplored. Existing methods often fail to address multiple user needs simultaneously, leading to suboptimal performance in real-world HCI scenarios. This is further exacerbated by the limitations of existing benchmarks, which are often narrow in scope and fail to capture the complexities of human-computer interactions. The paper aims to bridge this gap by exploring the complexities of HCI, specifically the different user needs, interaction styles, and interaction types. It investigates two different user needs, task-oriented and retrieval-related, to understand when and why user needs are not met. The paper builds on Pearl's causality framework to propose an interaction graph framework for HCI, which includes user actions and LLM actions. This framework captures the complexities of real-world HCI interactions, facilitating exploration and analysis. The paper uses this framework to create a new benchmark, U-CAN, which addresses the limitations of existing benchmarks and provides a more comprehensive evaluation of HCI performance. The goal is to develop a more robust and effective method for asking clarification questions that can better meet user needs in real-world HCI scenarios.

## Main Idea

The paper presents U-CAN (User-Guided Clarification for Asking Across Needs), a method that leverages interaction graphs to capture the complexities of human-computer interactions, aiming to meet multiple user needs in conversations. The paper introduces a new benchmark, U-CAN, which includes 491 Enrolled Student and 352 Tutor, designed to evaluate the effectiveness of HCI methods in addressing task-oriented and retrieval-related clarification. The study finds that existing methods show performance gaps between simulated and Enrolled Student settings, highlighting the need for user-guided clarification and interaction graph designs. The paper demonstrates that HCI can be facilitated by interaction graphs and proposes an interactive graph model to further improve HCI performance.

